### What happens?

I'm having a hard time to come up with a trivial example to reproduce the issue, but it's an easy fix. I'm developing an extension using the C API and have a custom aggregate function. In the C API implementation at https://github.com/duckdb/duckdb/blob/3a3967aa8190d0a2d1931d4ca4f5d920760030b4/src/main/capi/aggregate_function-c.cpp#L102 it's assumed that the vector contains `count` items, but if it's a constant vector it only contains one item. So there's a mismatch between the counts and hence many of the items will be pointing to undefined memory.

Adding a callback for a simple update fixes the issue without breaking the API. Here's an example even though it's mostly a 1-1 copy of the code in the _normal_ update function and the common functionality could be factored out into its own function.

```cpp
void CAPIAggregateSimpleUpdate(Vector inputs[], AggregateInputData &aggr_input_data, idx_t input_count,
                               data_ptr_t state, idx_t count) {
	DataChunk chunk;
	for (idx_t c = 0; c < input_count; c++) {
		inputs[c].Flatten(count);
		chunk.data.emplace_back(inputs[c]);
	}
	chunk.SetCardinality(count);

	auto &bind_data = aggr_input_data.bind_data->Cast<CAggregateFunctionBindData>();
	// Create a vector of count state items.
	vector<duckdb_aggregate_state> state_data(count, reinterpret_cast<duckdb_aggregate_state>(state));
	auto c_input_chunk = reinterpret_cast<duckdb_data_chunk>(&chunk);

	CAggregateExecuteInfo exec_info(bind_data.info);
	auto c_function_info = reinterpret_cast<duckdb_function_info>(&exec_info);
	bind_data.info.update(c_function_info, c_input_chunk, state_data.data());
	if (!exec_info.success) {
		throw InvalidInputException(exec_info.error);
	}
}
```

### To Reproduce

As I wrote above I have a hard time coming up with a reproducible example, but for me when it crashes it involves summing in a window function.

