## What

`DuckTransaction::Commit()` is declared `noexcept`, but its `catch (std::exception&)` block calls `undo_buffer.RevertCommit(...)` and `commit_state->RevertCommit()` **without an inner try/catch**. If either of those `RevertCommit` calls throws (e.g. `OutOfMemoryException`, `InternalException`), the second exception escapes the `noexcept` boundary and triggers `std::terminate()` → `abort()`, killing the entire process.

This is the **same bug-class** that PR #15903 ("Avoid throwing in catch block for failed commits") fixed in `WriteToWAL()`. The fix added an inner try/catch and the explanatory comment _"Ignore this error. If we fail to RevertCommit(), just return the original exception"_. The symmetric path in `Commit()` was missed.

## Where

File: `src/transaction/duck_transaction.cpp` (main HEAD)

The protected path (`WriteToWAL()`, lines 206-236):

```cpp
ErrorData DuckTransaction::WriteToWAL(...) noexcept {
    ErrorData error_data;
    try { ... } catch (std::exception &ex) {
        // Call RevertCommit() outside this try-catch as it itself may throw
        error_data = ErrorData(ex);
    }
    if (commit_state && error_data.HasError()) {
        try {
            commit_state->RevertCommit();              // ✅ wrapped
            commit_state.reset();
        } catch (std::exception &) {
            // Ignore this error. If we fail to RevertCommit(), just return the original exception
        }
    }
    return error_data;
}
```

The unprotected path (`Commit()`, lines 238-268):

```cpp
ErrorData DuckTransaction::Commit(AttachedDatabase &db, transaction_t new_commit_id,
                                  unique_ptr<StorageCommitState> commit_state) noexcept {
    ...
    UndoBuffer::IteratorState iterator_state;
    try {
        storage->Commit(commit_state.get());
        undo_buffer.Commit(iterator_state, commit_id);
        ...
        return ErrorData();
    } catch (std::exception &ex) {
        undo_buffer.RevertCommit(iterator_state, this->transaction_id);   // ❌ NOT wrapped
        if (commit_state) {
            commit_state->RevertCommit();                                  // ❌ NOT wrapped
        }
        return ErrorData(ex);
    }
}
```

## Real-world reproduction

We hit this in production on v1.4.4 (commit `6ddac802ff`) twice within 6 hours on different pods. Crash signature:

```
abort() ← __cxa_call_terminate ← __gxx_personality_v0
  ← _Unwind_Resume
  ← UndoBuffer::RevertCommit [clone .cold]
  ← DuckTransaction::Commit [clone .cold]
  ← DuckTransactionManager::CommitTransaction
  ← MetaTransaction::Commit
  ← ClientContext::EndQueryInternal
  ← duckdb_execute_pending
```

**First exception** (from `storage->Commit` / `undo_buffer.Commit`):
```
OutOfMemoryException: could not allocate block of size 256.0 KiB (18.6 GiB/18.6 GiB used)
```

**Second exception** (from `undo_buffer.RevertCommit` → `DataTable::RevertAppend` → `ColumnData::RevertAppend` → `SegmentTree::TryGetSegmentIndex`):
```
InternalException: Segment tree index not found for row number 367626
Segments:Start 368640 Count 0
```

(I am filing the SegmentTree underflow as a separate issue — that's the upstream trigger. This issue focuses on the noexcept boundary that turns any such second-throw into a process abort.)

## Proposed fix (5 lines)

Mirror the WAL-path treatment from #15903:

```diff
@@ -258,11 +258,18 @@ ErrorData DuckTransaction::Commit(AttachedDatabase &db, ...) noexcept {
         return ErrorData();
     } catch (std::exception &ex) {
-        undo_buffer.RevertCommit(iterator_state, this->transaction_id);
-        if (commit_state) {
-            // if we have written to the WAL - truncate the WAL on failure
-            commit_state->RevertCommit();
+        try {
+            undo_buffer.RevertCommit(iterator_state, this->transaction_id);
+        } catch (std::exception &) {
+            // Ignore this error. If we fail to RevertCommit(),
+            // just return the original exception.
+        }
+        if (commit_state) {
+            try {
+                commit_state->RevertCommit();
+            } catch (std::exception &) {}
         }
         return ErrorData(ex);
     }
```

The original exception's information is preserved via `return ErrorData(ex)`, so no diagnostics are lost. Behavior changes only on the failed-rollback path: instead of terminating the process, the original commit error is returned to the caller.

I am happy to send a PR if this approach looks right to maintainers.

## Affected versions

- v1.4.4 (confirmed reproduction)
- main HEAD as of 2026-05 (verified — bug still present)
- Likely all versions since #8997 introduced the `RevertCommit` call in this catch block

