I really hope that TypeScript can deal with circular references somehow because I really want to port DataForge to TypeScript!

In general, tsc allows all the operators on any types, except certain specific cases, like `a + b` where `a` and `b` are boolean values. There are cases when disabling certain operators the same way would be useful:

``` ts
// js doesn't have 64 bit numbers,
// so here is a clumsy workaround
interface int64 {
  [0]: number;
  [1]: number;
}

const a: int64 = ...;
const b: int64 = ...;

if (a == b) {
  ...
}
```

The `a == b` check is a mistake that will be hard to catch later. It would be nice to disable the `==` operator (as well as others) for this `int64` type.

