Type isn't get inferred properly in the context of mapped types for a single function with generic and multiple arguments using the same generic.
Not really sure how to describe the issue, the problem is clearly demonstrated in the playground.
Feel free to edit my message to put the relevant info and terminology for other people.

### 🕗 Version & Regression Information

All versions, including latest `4.9.5`.

### ⏯ Playground Link

[Playground link](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#-PxoAhYQYAMLgKAkcxWGz2YyOL4rAA0iOKhiWtXCU1mSBhF2J6CGYwBQIpu3BpDwBJRKz8yPp6PSWMs1jsDm59JcQ0RtOESF2fjJbKpMzmErhiXcFURVVl5HliNG-whUIQfngyCJaERgmEVOhvmyuXyhQ+fgkpGksnk8StAHo-c9nW8iuxStA8MUEBBSABydg7Y7IaDsWYphzaeLGznmxBIeJ+dZsdhCEQkB1B16usNId1SGRyMS+6ABysu941xPIGCpkwGYyZoA).

### 💻 Code

```ts
class User {
    id!: number
    name!: string
    status!: "invited" | "member"
}

type ObjectType<T> = new (...args: any[]) => T

class EqualOperator<T> {
    constructor(public value: T) {
    }
}

type Where<Entity> = {
    [P in keyof Entity]?: Entity[P] | EqualOperator<Entity[P]>
}

function findOne<Entity>(
    entityClass: ObjectType<Entity>,
    options: Where<Entity>,
) {}

function find<Entity>(entityClass: ObjectType<Entity>) {
    return (options: Where<Entity>) => {
        return
    }
}

findOne(User, {
    status: new EqualOperator("invited"), // EqualOperator doesn't infer the type
})

find(User)({
    status: new EqualOperator("invited"), // EqualOperator infers the type
})
```

### 🙁 Actual behavior

`EqualOperator` does infer string literal / enum type when used in the context of Entity[P] only for the second function.

### 🙂 Expected behavior

`EqualOperator` to infer string literal / enum type when used in the context of Entity[P] for the first function as well.

I would like the ability to do string manipulation within my types. Examples could include stripping out properties that match a regex/prefix/suffix or mapping types to include additional properties derived from the keys of existing ones.

Some examples might explain best:

**Example**

```ts
declare loadThings<T>(T): PossiblyLoading<T>;
declare getTheBar(id: number): Loader<Bar>;

let foo: { bar: Bar | undefined, barLoading: boolean };

foo = loadThings({
  bar: getTheBar(55302),
});

```

Where `PossiblyLoading` might be implemented like so:

```ts
type PossiblyLoading<T> = {
  [K in keyof T]: UnwrapLoader<T[K]>;
  [(K in keyof T) + "Loading"] + ?: boolean
}
```

Both should work because the string literal is easily detectable vs the token `?`.

This should also work with generic types that extend string:

```ts
type MutateProps<T, P extends string, S extends string> = {
  [P + (K in keyof T) + S] + ?: T[K]
}
```

But I would also want to be able to use these within conditional types, using standard javascript functions:

```ts
type RemovePrefixed<T, P extends string> = {
  [K in keyof T]: K.startsWith(P) ? never : T[K];
}
```

And we could even remove a prefix:
```ts
type RemovePrefix<T, P extends string> = {
  [(K in keyof T).startsWith(P) ? K.slice(P.length) : K]: T[K];
}
```

If we wanted to avoid getting too crazy with function calls we could perhaps do it like this:
```ts
type RemovePrefixed<T, P extends string> = {
  [(P + infer K) in keyof T ? never : K]: T[K];
}

type RemovePrefix<T, P extends string> = {
  [(P + infer K) in keyof T ? K : never]: T[P + K];
}
```

Although I think the one that supports a subset of string function is more broadly useful. For example, we could probably do something around indexing into "foo.bar.baz" and resolve the innermost type of baz.

